# @indipsyno/esi

An opinionated typescript API client for the EvE Online API (ESI). 

## Features

## Installation
Install the package from npm:
```
npm install @indipsyno/esi
```
## Usage
Before using the client, make sure the required user agent environment variable is set in your .env file:
```
ESI_USER_AGENT="My user agent"
```

This user agent will be appended with details of the client package, so to avoid duplicating details do not mention this in your user agent.

For more information on what you should put in your user agent see the [ESI Documentation](https://developers.eveonline.com/docs/services/esi/best-practices/#user-agents)

For other environment variables please see the [configuration section](#config)

In most use cases, you can use the `getEsiClient` function. It takes care of creating and reusing a singleton client with a default configuration suitable for most applications.

```typescript
import { getEsiClient } from '@indipsyno/esi'

const esi = getEsiClient()
```

More advanced use cases, you may want to create and manage your own client instance. The `EsiClient` class is exported directly for this purpose:

```typescript
import { EsiClient } from '@indipsyno/esi'

const esi = new EsiClient()
```

The `EsiClient` class supports several configuration options that let you customize its behavior. See the [configuration section](#config) for further details:

```typescript
import { EsiClient } from "@indipsyno/esi";

const esi = new EsiClient({
    bypassCache: false, // boolean, default: false
    middleware: [], // Middleware[], adds custom middleware to client
    retries: 3, // number, default: 3
    userAgentOverride: "Override" // string, overrides whole user agent string
})
```
>[!WARNING]
>Store your `EsiClient` as a singleton. Caching and rate limiting only apply within a single instance. While it’s possible to use separate clients for completely distinct use cases, this should be minimized to avoid unnecessary overhead.

All API methods are available through the `EsiClient` instance. For example:

```typescript
import { getEsiClient, EsiResponse, StatusGet } from '@indipsyno/esi'

const esi = getEsiClient()

const status : EsiResponse<StatusGet> = await esi.statusApi.getStatus();
```

When a request succeeds an `EsiResponse<T>` will be returned, or for a non-2xx status code an `EsiError` will be thrown.

```typescript
interface EsiResponse<TData> {
    data: TData;
    headers: Record<string, string>;
    status: number;
}

interface EsiError {
    error: string;
    response: Response;
    status: number;
}
```

All methods are fully typed, so will take a parameters object and return a corresponding object as the `EsiResponse` data.

```typescript
import { getEsiClient, EsiResponse, CharacterGet } from '@indipsyno/esi'

const esi = getEsiClient()

const character : EsiResponse<CharacterGet> = await esi.characterApi.getCharacter({
    characterId: 9000000
})
```

